module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

// Consumer: directory-derived recurring_failure_mode.roster, projected by
// gunbc.design_ledgers.expected_design_failure_modes_md through docs_projection_gate.
data the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: an explicit `as` cast the typechecker Accepts but the evaluator refuses, so what the cast admits has two authorities (DESIGN section 3): v1.compiler.infer validate_cast abstains whenever either side is outside std.coercion dag_cast_rules, and the seed interpreter's eval_cast decides the cast alone at runtime. HARM: a checked program crashes on a construct its checker blessed, which is below the section 4b floor.",
    "SPECIMENS: `n as Nat` in gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_boot_diagnostic_bundle console_size_of_stat aborted an mtcollins1 boot diagnostic with `TypeError: cannot cast Int to Nat` (CI run 35871654468) although the value was guarded by `n < 0`; runner_microvm_cell_readiness_store decode_attempt hit the same class earlier. The sweep at the fix found three more unguarded Int-as-Nat sites (gunbc.ci_workflow_scan_producer scan walk bound, gunbc.harness.harness_cli harness_release_cli ceiling and generation).",
    "RESOLUTION FOR Int -> Nat: it is a REFINEMENT cast, not a re-typing, because a total meaning would have to clamp a negative, which std.checked_arithmetic already names as the section 5 absorbing fallback. std.coercion refinement_cast_rules is the one authority: validate_cast refuses a cast over one of its rows at compile time, eval_cast refuses the same rows as an unreachable echo, and std.checked_arithmetic checked_int_to_nat (promoted from gunbc.spark.serving_load_probe) is the sanctioned crossing. A nonnegative literal needs no cast and a Nat-typed value casts by identity.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the defect is a checker/evaluator disagreement on cast admission, not a spelling mismatch on an identity cast (identity_cast_compares_type_spellings) and not a missing refusal at the checker alone. FOUND AT MITIGATABLE (a located runtime TypeError). NOW, for refinement_cast_rules rows: STRUCTURALLY GUARANTEED on the source-to-interpretation path. `Bool as Int` is no longer a dag_cast_rules row (a `Bool -> Bool` identity keeps Bool in the cast domain), so validate_cast refuses it at compile time. Two companions landed with it, read from the same rows: std.coercion dag_subtraction_result_type types a difference of refinement values at the row's source type, so `(a - b) as Nat` over two Nats is a refinement cast rather than an identity; and std.algebra count / length return Nat. RESIDUE, stated rather than claimed away: a cast outside the dag_cast_rules domain is still decided by eval alone; the IMPLICIT grounded Int -> Nat identity (grounded_primitive_coproduct_identities) still admits an unproven Int in a Nat return, argument or field position with no check -- its wall is staged next, measured at identity grain before it lands. CEILING: STRUCTURALLY GUARANTEED -- cast admission is decidable. NEXT TRIGGER: eval_cast's arms derive from std.coercion rows (one table read by both layers) so no cast outside the checker's decision reaches a runtime refusal.",
  ],
  evidence: [],
}
